WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.960
You know the feeling you get
when you learn something new

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:06.360
about a health problem
you’ve been trying to reverse?

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:09.780
Maybe high blood pressure,
diabetes, or heart disease.

00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:13.500
Well, there’s nothing I like better
than bringing you the information

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:16.260
that will help you do just that.

00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:18.780
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast.

00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:21.660
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:24.900
Today we begin a new series
with the latest research

00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:26.340
on a variety of topics.

00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:28.380
First up, we look at
the benefits of vinegar

00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:31.734
for blood sugar control
and polycystic ovary treatment.

00:00:32.097 --> 00:00:36.180
The use of vinegar for weight
loss dates back to the 1700’s.

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:39.420
I did a whole chapter about
it in my book How Not to Diet

00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:43.920
entitled Amping AMPK, the fat-
controller enzyme in the body,

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:46.320
which appears to be the
mechanism behind the multitude

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:48.000
of beneficial metabolic effects.

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:52.680
In my 21 tweaks to accelerate weight
loss I recommend 2 teaspoons

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:54.720
with every meal, which
is considered safe,

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:58.140
though it should be added
to food or diluted in water,

00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:01.560
never consumed straight, and
diabetics should make sure

00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:03.840
the vinegar doesn’t make
their blood sugars go too low.

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:06.300
But that’s one of
the benefits of vinegar,

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:09.540
blunting the blood sugar and
insulin spikes after a meal in both

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:12.840
healthy individuals and those with
blood sugar disorders like diabetes.

00:01:13.380 --> 00:01:17.040
In a systematic review and meta-
analysis of interventional trials

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:18.720
of the effects of
vinegar consumption

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:22.380
on blood sugar control in
type 2 diabetes, vinegar improves

00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:25.560
both short-term and longer-term
measures of blood sugar control

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:29.100
and as like a little
side bonus resulted in

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:31.620
a remarkable reduction in
cholesterol to boot.

00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:35.040
And it didn’t seem to matter whether
it was apple cider vinegar or not.

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:39.600
All vinegar by definition has
the active ingredient, acetic acid.

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:42.480
So, what are the implications
for clinical practice?

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:45.660
Well, clinicians could
incorporate vinegar consumption

00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:48.660
as part of their dietary advice
for patients with diabetes

00:01:48.660 --> 00:01:51.300
with the caution that it
can work a little too good

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:54.240
and so you have to monitor closely
to make sure diabetic patients

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:57.300
aren’t overmedicated and
bottom out their sugars.

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:01.860
In my video on water purification,
I talked about this study

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:05.820
showing that the cheapest and best
way to disinfect your toothbrush

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:08.820
so you don’t have to keep
buying new ones maybe to soak it

00:02:08.820 --> 00:02:11.400
for 10 minutes in 50%
white vinegar and water.

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:18.060
So what about a vinegar sock soak
for athlete’s foot or toenail fungus?

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:22.920
Foot soaks in half vinegar/half water
can help antifungals work better,

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:26.400
but inconvenience of tub
soaks limits compliance.

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:30.000
So, how about pouring vinegar
and water on the toes of your socks

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:32.580
and then just like putting your
feet up for a little while.

00:02:32.580 --> 00:02:35.520
Not sure how that would
be any more convenient,

00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:37.800
but seemed to work in
this clinician’s practice.

00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:41.160
Instead of vinegar for
just an athlete’s foot,

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:43.680
what about vinegar
for the whole athlete?

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:45.840
“The Eﬀect of Vinegar
Supplementation on

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:47.760
High-Intensity
Cycling Performance.”

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:51.240
They randomized people to do
2 tablespoons of diluted vinegar,

00:02:51.240 --> 00:02:56.760
or sugar, or both, or neither
and the vinegar did nothing.

00:02:57.480 --> 00:03:00.420
Is there anything else
vinegar does help with?

00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:03.780
The Intake of a Vinegar
Beverage Is Associated

00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:07.500
with Restoration of
Ovulatory Function in Women

00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:09.660
with Polycystic Ovary Syndrome.

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:14.940
For those of you unfamiliar,
PCOS is a major cause

00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:16.860
of irregular menstruation,

00:03:16.860 --> 00:03:19.980
and since insulin resistance
has been suggested to be

00:03:19.980 --> 00:03:22.020
one of the causes of
polycystic ovaries,

00:03:22.020 --> 00:03:24.540
and we know vinegar can
improve insulin resistance,

00:03:24.540 --> 00:03:28.440
researchers decided to study
the effect of vinegar by giving

00:03:28.440 --> 00:03:31.740
seven patients seeking a
non-pharmacological treatment

00:03:31.740 --> 00:03:35.520
for PCOS, a beverage
containing a tablespoon

00:03:35.520 --> 00:03:38.700
of apple cider vinegar
daily for a few months.

00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:42.060
The insulin resistance
did improve in most,

00:03:42.060 --> 00:03:44.100
but did their cycles return?

00:03:44.100 --> 00:03:46.500
Yes, in 4 out of 7.

00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:50.700
Before the study they were
either only having their period

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:54.180
every 50 days or so or none
at all or just not ovulating,

00:03:54.180 --> 00:03:58.260
but most resumed within
40 days on the vinegar.

00:03:58.860 --> 00:04:02.160
Now, this doesn’t prove anything,
but it’s at least sufficient

00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:04.740
to indicate the possibility
that it may help.

00:04:04.740 --> 00:04:06.240
And what’s the downside?

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:09.780
A tablespoon of vinegar would
cost less than 10 cents a day.

00:04:09.780 --> 00:04:14.280
Just remember, never drink
vinegar straight, as it can cause

00:04:14.280 --> 00:04:18.000
second degree caustic
burns down your throat.

00:04:19.380 --> 00:04:24.840
Next up, we discover how coffee
and tea may impair iron absorption,

00:04:24.840 --> 00:04:29.255
which may actually help explain
some of their benefits.

00:04:30.060 --> 00:04:33.840
In my videos on tea, I caution
not to drink with meals,

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:36.960
because it can inhibit the absorption
of iron from foods

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:39.420
anywhere from 26 to 99 percent,

00:04:39.420 --> 00:04:43.080
perhaps depending on the brewing time,
brand, or how strong it is.

00:04:43.080 --> 00:04:47.280
What about the inhibition
of food iron absorption by coffee?

00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:50.940
When I was first looking this up,
I ran across this study

00:04:50.940 --> 00:04:53.280
on the effects of discontinuing
coffee intake

00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:56.040
on the iron status
of Guatemalan toddlers.

00:04:56.040 --> 00:04:59.580
I’m thinking they must be talking
about breastfeeding mothers

00:04:59.580 --> 00:05:02.880
or something, but no, coffee
is one of the first liquids

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:04.440
given to infants in Guatemala.

00:05:04.440 --> 00:05:05.520
I did not know that!

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:10.860
Anyway, the inhibition
of food iron absorption by coffee.

00:05:10.860 --> 00:05:14.460
A cup of coffee reduced iron absorption
from a hamburger meal

00:05:14.460 --> 00:05:18.000
by 39 percent, as opposed
to a 64 percent decrease with tea.

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:21.120
So, not as bad as tea,
but still significant blockage.

00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:23.580
And just like with the tea,
the stronger the coffee,

00:05:23.580 --> 00:05:26.400
the more iron absorption
was impeded.

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:29.760
In terms of timing, no decrease
in iron absorption occurred

00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:32.340
when coffee was consumed
an hour before a meal,

00:05:32.340 --> 00:05:34.020
but waiting an hour
after the meal

00:05:34.020 --> 00:05:35.760
to drink the coffee
didn’t seem to help,

00:05:35.760 --> 00:05:37.860
which can be probably
explained by the fact

00:05:37.860 --> 00:05:40.440
that it may take
up to nearly two hours

00:05:40.440 --> 00:05:42.180
to clear a meal
from the stomach;

00:05:42.180 --> 00:05:43.680
so, starting an hour in,

00:05:43.680 --> 00:05:45.420
the coffee can catch up
to the food.

00:05:46.320 --> 00:05:49.020
Now, you can reverse
the effect of tea or coffee

00:05:49.020 --> 00:05:51.780
by adding orange juice to a meal,
or even better an orange,

00:05:51.780 --> 00:05:53.940
or any source
of vitamin C-rich food.

00:05:53.940 --> 00:05:56.640
The vitamin C
boosts iron absorption,

00:05:56.640 --> 00:05:59.100
which is good for people
who are borderline anemic,

00:05:59.100 --> 00:06:03.240
but for many, the blockage
of iron absorption from coffee

00:06:03.240 --> 00:06:05.700
may actually be a good thing.

00:06:05.700 --> 00:06:08.460
Iron is a double-edged sword.

00:06:08.460 --> 00:06:11.220
On the one hand, iron
is an essential element

00:06:11.220 --> 00:06:12.780
vital for blood production.

00:06:12.780 --> 00:06:15.840
On the other hand, iron
is a potent pro-oxidant.

00:06:15.840 --> 00:06:19.320
So, maybe the reduced risk
of type 2 diabetes

00:06:19.320 --> 00:06:23.460
associated with coffee consumption
is due to the inhibition

00:06:23.460 --> 00:06:25.200
of iron absorption by coffee.

00:06:25.200 --> 00:06:29.460
See, the risk of type 2 diabetes
increases with the increase

00:06:29.460 --> 00:06:31.320
in the amount of ferritin
in your blood,

00:06:31.320 --> 00:06:33.180
which is a measure
of your iron stores.

00:06:33.180 --> 00:06:37.069
So, higher iron stores,
higher diabetes risk.

00:06:37.069 --> 00:06:38.280
It’s the same thing with the risk

00:06:38.280 --> 00:06:40.140
of gestational diabetes
during pregnancy.

00:06:40.140 --> 00:06:42.780
So, you need to make sure
you’re getting enough iron,

00:06:42.780 --> 00:06:45.420
but not building up
too much iron in your body.

00:06:45.960 --> 00:06:48.240
How do we know
it’s cause and effect?

00:06:48.240 --> 00:06:50.820
Because if you randomize diabetics

00:06:50.820 --> 00:06:52.560
like old-fashioned bloodletting,

00:06:52.560 --> 00:06:54.900
but instead of leeches,
they just donate blood

00:06:54.900 --> 00:06:56.700
a couple times to lower
their iron stores,

00:06:56.700 --> 00:06:58.740
and those in the blood donation group

00:06:58.740 --> 00:07:01.800
had better blood sugar control,
better insulin secretion,

00:07:01.800 --> 00:07:03.240
less insulin resistance.

00:07:04.200 --> 00:07:06.540
Iron depletion improves
artery dysfunction

00:07:06.540 --> 00:07:07.980
in type 2 diabetics as well.

00:07:07.980 --> 00:07:10.080
It’s the same thing with gout.

00:07:10.080 --> 00:07:13.320
Does inhibition of iron absorption
by coffee reduce

00:07:13.320 --> 00:07:15.660
the risk of gout? Let’s find out.

00:07:15.660 --> 00:07:20.220
Near-iron deﬁciency-induced
remission of gouty arthritis.

00:07:20.220 --> 00:07:23.040
They took gout patients
and maintained their iron stores

00:07:23.040 --> 00:07:26.520
at the lowest level possible
without causing anemia and…

00:07:26.520 --> 00:07:29.880
gouty attacks markedly diminished
in every patient,

00:07:29.880 --> 00:07:32.520
with effects ranging
from a complete remission

00:07:32.520 --> 00:07:35.900
to a marked reduction of incidence
and severity of gouty attacks.

00:07:35.940 --> 00:07:38.520
So, maybe that’s one reason
coffee consumption

00:07:38.520 --> 00:07:40.200
is associated
with lower risk of gout.

00:07:40.200 --> 00:07:42.420
It blocks some of the iron uptake.

00:07:43.260 --> 00:07:46.860
Increasing evidence suggests
that iron is also involved

00:07:46.860 --> 00:07:50.460
in multiple diseases of aging,
including Alzheimer's disease,

00:07:50.460 --> 00:07:52.680
Parkinson’s disease,
and heart disease.

00:07:52.680 --> 00:07:54.840
In terms of iron and Alzheimer’s,

00:07:54.840 --> 00:07:58.860
they think it’s iron’s potential
to effectively “rust” brain tissue

00:07:58.860 --> 00:08:02.220
by producing free radicals
that may cause neurodegeneration,

00:08:02.220 --> 00:08:04.800
contributing to Alzheimer’s
disease pathology

00:08:04.800 --> 00:08:07.740
at numerous levels, so much so
that there is interest

00:08:07.740 --> 00:08:10.800
in trying to treat Alzheimer's disease
by targeting iron.

00:08:11.460 --> 00:08:15.540
High body iron stores may also
be associated with shorter telomeres,

00:08:15.540 --> 00:08:17.880
which is a biomarker
of biological aging.

00:08:17.880 --> 00:08:21.600
And for those of you thinking,
well if I had high iron stores,

00:08:21.600 --> 00:08:22.800
I’d probably know about it.

00:08:22.800 --> 00:08:26.280
Only 5 percent of patients
with elevated iron report

00:08:26.280 --> 00:08:28.740
ever being told by a doctor
that this was the case.

00:08:29.460 --> 00:08:33.540
To prevent too much iron accumulation,
regularly drinking tea

00:08:33.540 --> 00:08:36.120
with meals will do it,
found to decrease the amount

00:08:36.120 --> 00:08:37.860
of bloodletting you
have to do for people

00:08:37.860 --> 00:08:40.200
with a genetic iron overload disease.

00:08:40.200 --> 00:08:44.700
But you’d want to do the opposite—
drink tea only apart from meals—

00:08:44.700 --> 00:08:47.040
if you were at risk
for iron deficiency anemia.

00:08:47.760 --> 00:08:50.640
Besides tea and coffee,
there are other beverages

00:08:50.640 --> 00:08:52.080
that can block iron absorption.

00:08:52.080 --> 00:08:54.720
Peppermint is right up there
with black tea,

00:08:54.720 --> 00:08:56.880
chocolate milk would do it too,

00:08:56.880 --> 00:09:00.540
and chamomile blocks iron
about in the same range as coffee.

00:09:00.540 --> 00:09:04.440
So, if your iron stores are high,
these are great mealtime choices.

00:09:04.440 --> 00:09:06.720
If you’re struggling
to get enough iron,

00:09:06.720 --> 00:09:09.780
you wouldn’t want to regularly drink
these with meals.

00:09:09.780 --> 00:09:13.020
We think it’s
the polyphenol phytonutrients.

00:09:13.020 --> 00:09:14.940
So, what about herbs and spices?

00:09:14.940 --> 00:09:17.280
They’re packed
with polyphenol goodness.

00:09:17.280 --> 00:09:20.820
Now, this study was done
basically in a test tube,

00:09:20.820 --> 00:09:24.000
not in real people,
but there is this case report

00:09:24.000 --> 00:09:27.060
of iron deficiency anemia
due to high-dose turmeric.

00:09:27.060 --> 00:09:31.200
A physician treated himself
for an osteoarthritis flare

00:09:31.200 --> 00:09:34.680
with capsules of turmeric extract
and he was anemic,

00:09:34.680 --> 00:09:37.320
couldn’t get his iron up
despite taking iron supplements.

00:09:37.320 --> 00:09:39.780
But two weeks
after stopping the turmeric,

00:09:39.780 --> 00:09:42.300
his blood count and iron levels
were all back to normal.

00:09:42.300 --> 00:09:45.900
So, on one hand, those presenting
to doctor’s offices

00:09:45.900 --> 00:09:49.320
with iron deficiency anemia
should be asked about supplement use;

00:09:49.320 --> 00:09:52.020
at the same time,
the ability of turmeric

00:09:52.020 --> 00:09:54.900
to glom on to intestinal iron
may lead to it being useful

00:09:54.900 --> 00:09:57.537
in states of iron overload.

00:09:58.020 --> 00:10:01.200
Finally today, we look at how
a single meal high in saturated fat

00:10:01.200 --> 00:10:03.178
can impair cognition.

00:10:03.562 --> 00:10:07.140
Having a high cholesterol level
in your early 40s is associated

00:10:07.140 --> 00:10:11.580
with an increased risk of Alzheimer's
and vascular dementia decades later.

00:10:11.580 --> 00:10:16.980
And your cholesterol doesn’t have to be
that high to increase dementia risk.

00:10:16.980 --> 00:10:20.460
A 57 percent higher risk
of developing Alzheimer's

00:10:20.460 --> 00:10:24.600
at a total cholesterol of 240
compared to under 200.

00:10:24.600 --> 00:10:28.020
I’ve done a bunch of videos
talking about the clogging

00:10:28.020 --> 00:10:30.300
of the cerebral arteries
inside of the brain

00:10:30.300 --> 00:10:34.140
with the same atherosclerosis
that causes heart attacks and strokes,

00:10:34.140 --> 00:10:37.380
choking off blood flow to the
memory centers in the brain.

00:10:38.040 --> 00:10:41.280
This may be why higher
saturated fat intake was found

00:10:41.280 --> 00:10:45.420
to be associated with the development
of worse global cognitive function

00:10:45.420 --> 00:10:49.020
and memory decline, an apparent
acceleration of brain aging

00:10:49.020 --> 00:10:50.760
as if you were six years older.

00:10:51.420 --> 00:10:54.360
How can we cut down
on saturated fat?

00:10:54.360 --> 00:10:57.480
By cutting down on cheese,
cake, ice cream, and chicken,

00:10:57.480 --> 00:11:00.060
essentially the top
sources in the American diet,

00:11:00.060 --> 00:11:02.760
but also pork, burgers,
and beef in general.

00:11:03.660 --> 00:11:07.440
In my video on the role of glycotoxins
in cognitive decline,

00:11:07.440 --> 00:11:10.980
I profiled this study, showing
that just five days of a high-fat,

00:11:10.980 --> 00:11:13.920
low-carb diet impairs
energy metabolism in the heart

00:11:13.920 --> 00:11:18.180
as well as cognitive function,
concluding such diets

00:11:18.180 --> 00:11:20.280
are detrimental to the
heart and brain.

00:11:20.280 --> 00:11:22.800
Now, they were thinking
the impaired energy production

00:11:22.800 --> 00:11:25.260
may have accounted for the
brain dysfunction as well,

00:11:25.260 --> 00:11:28.680
but high levels of dietary
glycotoxins, so-called AGE’s,

00:11:28.680 --> 00:11:30.900
are also associated with
cognitive decline,

00:11:30.900 --> 00:11:33.240
so we may want
to cut down our intake

00:11:33.240 --> 00:11:36.120
of baked, broiled, or grilled meat,
chicken, or fish,

00:11:36.120 --> 00:11:38.100
as well as certain
dairy products.

00:11:38.100 --> 00:11:40.620
But there’s another possibility.

00:11:40.620 --> 00:11:46.500
“Metabolic Endotoxemia:
A Potential Underlying Mechanism

00:11:46.500 --> 00:11:49.560
of the Relationship
between Dietary Fat Intake

00:11:49.560 --> 00:11:52.140
and Risk for Cognitive
Impairments.”

00:11:52.140 --> 00:11:55.260
Endotoxins are highly
pro-inflammatory components

00:11:55.260 --> 00:11:57.660
of certain types of
bacteria like E. coli

00:11:57.660 --> 00:11:59.820
that are released
when bacteria die,

00:11:59.820 --> 00:12:02.220
and can end up being absorbed
through our gut wall

00:12:02.220 --> 00:12:06.180
and end up in the bloodstream,
resulting in what’s called endotoxemia.

00:12:06.180 --> 00:12:08.820
The highest levels of these
endotoxins are found,

00:12:08.820 --> 00:12:11.940
not surprisingly, in foods
like meat that are contaminated

00:12:11.940 --> 00:12:14.340
with bacteria, both
red meat and white meat.

00:12:14.340 --> 00:12:17.250
And having elevated levels of
endotoxin circulating

00:12:17.250 --> 00:12:21.060
in your blood stream may be
detrimental for healthy aging,

00:12:21.060 --> 00:12:23.340
associated with
a large range of diseases,

00:12:23.340 --> 00:12:27.480
such as obesity, type 2 diabetes,
heart disease, and depression.

00:12:28.140 --> 00:12:30.960
Put people on a high-fat,
low-carb diet for five days,

00:12:30.960 --> 00:12:33.720
and endotoxin levels in the blood stream
roughly double.

00:12:33.720 --> 00:12:37.020
Endotoxin levels before
and after a few days

00:12:37.020 --> 00:12:38.400
on a high-fat diet.

00:12:38.940 --> 00:12:41.460
In fact, even a single meal
can do it.

00:12:41.460 --> 00:12:43.920
Give people some sausage
and egg McMuffins

00:12:43.920 --> 00:12:46.380
with hash browns, and you
can get a significant increase

00:12:46.380 --> 00:12:48.360
within hours after the meal.

00:12:48.360 --> 00:12:52.800
How do we know it wasn’t the refined carbs
in the muffin or something?

00:12:52.800 --> 00:12:55.800
Because the control meal
had a muffin too,

00:12:55.800 --> 00:12:58.380
but lower fat
and no sausage and egg.

00:12:58.380 --> 00:13:01.380
In fact, you can have people
chug straight sugar water

00:13:01.380 --> 00:13:03.480
and no change in endotoxin levels,

00:13:03.480 --> 00:13:06.720
whereas there was a big jump
within hours drinking straight cream,

00:13:06.720 --> 00:13:08.160
which is pretty much straight fat.

00:13:08.160 --> 00:13:11.760
And not just any kind of fat,
but mostly saturated fat.

00:13:11.760 --> 00:13:14.850
A double-blind, randomized
crossover trial demonstrated

00:13:14.850 --> 00:13:17.460
that a single meal
high in saturated fat

00:13:17.460 --> 00:13:20.460
can impede attention
(meaning the cognitive ability

00:13:20.460 --> 00:13:24.120
to distinguish target stimuli
from distractors in a computer game),

00:13:24.120 --> 00:13:27.180
compared with an identical meal
high in the kind of fat

00:13:27.180 --> 00:13:29.040
found in nuts, seeds,
and avocados.

00:13:29.040 --> 00:13:33.060
And this cognitive deficit
was present five hours after the meal,

00:13:33.060 --> 00:13:35.460
and who knows how much
longer after that.

00:13:35.460 --> 00:13:39.600
There are two ways to cut down
on endotoxin bursts after meals.

00:13:39.600 --> 00:13:42.060
One is to not eat so many
in the first place.

00:13:42.060 --> 00:13:45.900
But if you do eat meat and dairy,
the addition of fiber-rich foods

00:13:45.900 --> 00:13:48.060
can blunt the endotoxin surge.

00:13:48.060 --> 00:13:51.840
Same sausage and
egg McMuffin endotoxin bomb

00:13:51.840 --> 00:13:54.480
but with or without Fiber
One cereal,

00:13:54.480 --> 00:13:56.700
which would be
like 30 grams of fiber,

00:13:56.700 --> 00:13:59.400
and it seemed
to glom onto the endotoxins,

00:13:59.400 --> 00:14:02.940
preventing the bump of endotoxemia
three hours after the meal.

00:14:02.940 --> 00:14:05.820
The fiber also reduced
the oxidative stress,

00:14:05.820 --> 00:14:08.100
the free radicals
generated by such a meal,

00:14:08.100 --> 00:14:12.780
showing clearly that the addition of fiber
to the high-fat, high-calorie meal

00:14:12.780 --> 00:14:15.000
had profound effects on metabolic

00:14:15.000 --> 00:14:16.980
and inflammatory events
after the meal.

00:14:16.980 --> 00:14:21.000
And over time, meal after meal,
this could have long-term implications.

00:14:21.840 --> 00:14:25.560
Recent investigations reported
higher abundance of endotoxins

00:14:25.560 --> 00:14:28.080
in the brains of patients
with Alzheimer’s disease

00:14:28.080 --> 00:14:32.760
on autopsy—endotoxins building up
in the memory center of the brain,

00:14:32.760 --> 00:14:34.800
the region of the brain
that develops the earliest

00:14:34.800 --> 00:14:37.380
and most profound neuropathology
in Alzheimer’s.

00:14:38.160 --> 00:14:42.240
Some advanced Alzheimer's patients
had up to a 26-fold increase

00:14:42.240 --> 00:14:45.660
in endotoxin levels over
age-matched controls without dementia.

00:14:45.660 --> 00:14:48.780
And where were the
endotoxins concentrated?

00:14:48.780 --> 00:14:52.560
Smack dab in the middle
of the amyloid plaques,

00:14:52.560 --> 00:14:55.080
a pathology characteristic
of Alzheimer's.

00:14:55.080 --> 00:15:00.120
For these and other reasons,
proper nutrition has been proposed

00:15:00.120 --> 00:15:01.980
as a promising
non-medical strategy

00:15:01.980 --> 00:15:05.671
to prevent cognitive decline
and subsequent dementia.

